---
// Lesson 4.1a — The Enemy: Waypoint Patrols
// EnemyController.PlatformerMove: startPosition, patrolDistance, flip.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.1a — The Enemy: Waypoint Patrols</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The turret never moves; the enemy hunts. Start with its calmest
    behavior: a guard walking a beat, back and forth, forever — a loop
    built from one saved position and one sign flip.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the patrol half of EnemyController.cs:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float speed = 2.0f;
[SerializeField] private float patrolDistance = 3.0f;
private Vector3 startPosition;
private int direction = 1;

private void Start()
{
    startPosition = transform.position;
    ...
}

private void PlatformerMove()
{
    transform.Translate(Vector2.right * direction * speed * Time.deltaTime);

    float dist = Vector3.Distance(startPosition, transform.position);
    if (dist &gt; patrolDistance)
    {
        direction *= -1; // Switch direction
        ...
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>Predict before running: the enemy starts at x = 5. Where does
    it turn around — at x = 8, x = 2, or both? Draw the walk as a line
    with turn-around points. Then: why does Start save
    startPosition — what would break if Update saved it instead?</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The patrol is a loop with no loop keyword — Translate every
            frame, flip when far. Where is the "repeat" hiding, and
            what would the AP exam's REPEAT UNTIL version of this look
            like?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — rebuild the patrol from a blank
    EnemyController.</strong> Comment out PlatformerMove's body and
    bring it back in order: remember start (Start) → walk
    (Translate with direction × speed × deltaTime — the movement
    pattern from <a href="1.4a.html">1.4a</a>, wearing a sign) →
    measure (Distance from start) → flip (direction *= -1). Run after
    each step; the walk is boring alone, the flip is the behavior.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the invisible leash.</strong> There are no
    markers in the scene — no waypoint gizmos, nothing. The enemy
    patrols <em>relative to where it spawned</em>: move the prefab
    instance in the scene and the beat moves too. patrolDistance is
    the leash length, a serialized knob like every other. What is the
    patrol shape if you set patrolDistance to 0? To 100?</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — direction as signed state.</strong> A single
    int, 1 or −1, is the enemy's entire memory of where it is going.
    Multiplying by −1 flips the sign — one line, no ifs. This is the
    trick behind every "which way am I facing" field in games; your
    sprite.flipX from <a href="4.2a.html">4.2a</a> reads this exact
    value.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The runaway guard</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Move the enemy mid-patrol in the Scene view while in
                PLAY mode. What is its new turnaround pattern, and why
                does it never find its old beat?</li>
            <li>Save startPosition in Update instead of Start. The
                enemy... what? Explain using the value startPosition
                holds on frame 2.</li>
            <li>Change direction *= -1 to direction = 1. What single
                behavior remains — and where does the enemy end up
                after ten seconds?</li>
            <li>Set speed to 20 and watch a full beat. Which side of
                the leash does the flip overshoot — and why does a
                big speed make the patrol longer than
                patrolDistance?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Design the beat</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Place two enemies with overlapping patrols guarding a
                coin. Where do the beats intersect, and can the player
                time the gap?</li>
            <li>Patrol on ONE axis only, by code. What would you change
                to make a vertical guard (an elevator enemy)?</li>
            <li>Hard: a patrol that pauses at each end for one second.
                Which pattern from <a href="3.3a.html">3.3a</a> do you
                reach for — and what is the "cooldown" here?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Rebuild the patrol: save start → translate → measure → flip</li>
            <li>Explain why Start saves the anchor and Update cannot</li>
            <li>Explain direction as signed state and the *= −1 flip</li>
            <li>Design patrol layouts (overlaps, leashes, choke points) on purpose</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — patrol, chase, and the state machine</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — the full reference</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.2a.html">4.2a — Patrol Polish: Bounds, Flips &amp; Animations</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.1a" title="4.1a The Enemy: Waypoint Patrols — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
